# adding a small constant to avoid division by zero\n# Feature description\n# This feature calculates the ratio of how compact the silhouette is relative to its elongatedness. Compactness and elongatedness are related to the shape and extent of the silhouette, so their relationship might help distinguish between different vehicle types (e.g., a bus might have different proportions compared to a van).\n# Usefulness: \n# This feature can capture the interplay between compactness and elongatedness, which might vary significantly across vehicle classes with different shapes.\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 2: Log transformation of "SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS"\n# Feature name\ndf[\'LOG_SCALED_VARIANCE_MINOR_AXIS\'] = np.log(df[\'SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS\'] + 1)  # log transformation with smoothing constant\n# Feature description\n# This applies a logarithmic transformation to the scaled variance along the minor axis to compress the magnitude of large variance values.\n# Usefulness: \n# Many machine learning algorithms benefit from normalized or compressed distributions of feature values. Scaled variance for the minor axis has a wide range (184 to 998), so log transformation helps make these values more comparable, avoiding potential dominance during modeling.\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 3: Z-score normalization of "MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY" and its interaction with "PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY"\n# Feature name\ndf[\'MAX_LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY_ZSCORE\'] = (df[\'MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY\'] - df[\'MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY\'].mean()) / df[\'MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY\'].std()\ndf[\'INTERACTION_PR_MAX_RECT\'] = df[\'MAX_LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY_ZSCORE\'] * df[\'PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY\']\n# Feature description\n# Z-score normalization brings "MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY" to a standard scale. For the interaction, we multiply it with "PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY" to capture interactions between major and minor axes of rectangularity.\n# Usefulness: \n# Interactions between axis-re